</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background and Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Masked Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Masked Generative Modeling (MGM) has emerged as an alternative to AR models for modeling sequences of discrete tokens. In MGM, sequences are partially masked during training, and the model learns to predict the masked tokens. Early works such as BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>)</cite> used MGM as a representation learning objective to extract semantically rich features for downstream tasks. Later works like MaskGIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and MUSE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> explored using MGM for generative modeling. In this setup, a sequence is initialized with only mask tokens, which are gradually unmasked by a neural network during sampling to generate the desired output. These works primarily focus on image generation tasks, where discrete image tokenizers such as VQGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Esser et al., 2021</a>)</cite> are used to convert images into sequences of discrete tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More recently,MDMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> have further developed the theory of MGM by formalizing the masking and unmasking process as the forward and reverse diffusion processes in discrete space. This provides a principled framework for training and sampling from these models. MDMs have renewed interest in masked modeling for language generation, offering theoretical advantages over AR models, such as better speed-quality tradeoffs and improved controllability. Notably, LLaDa-8B and Dream-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> demonstrated that MDMs can achieve competitive performance compared to AR models at scale. Several follow-up works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">You et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, including LaViDa and MMaDa, extend MDMs to multi-modal tasks such as image understanding and text-to-image generation. Their capabilities are summarized in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, given a sequence of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> discrete tokens <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}=[X_{0}^{1},X_{0}^{2},\dots,X_{0}^{L}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}=[X_{0}^{1},X_{0}^{2},\dots,X_{0}^{L}]</annotation></semantics></math>, the forward process <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(X_{t}|X_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(X_{t}|X_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> gradually masks the tokens over the time interval [0,1], with <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\geq t\geq s\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\geq t\geq s\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. At <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=[X_{1}^{1},X_{1}^{2},\dots,X_{1}^{L}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=[X_{1}^{1},X_{1}^{2},\dots,X_{1}^{L}]</annotation></semantics></math> consists entirely of masked tokens, denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[M]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[M]</annotation></semantics></math>. A neural network <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to model the reverse process <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(X_{s}|X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(X_{s}|X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. The masked diffusion objective is defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MDM}}=-\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{0},X_{t}}\left[\frac{1}{t}\log p_{\theta}(X_{0}|X_{t})\right]=-\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{0},X_{t}}\left[\frac{1}{t}\sum_{i\in\{1,2,\dots,L\},X_{t}^{i}=[M]}\log p_{\theta}(X_{0}^{i}|X_{t})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MDM</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MDM}}=-\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{0},X_{t}}\left[\frac{1}{t}\log p_{\theta}(X_{0}|X_{t})\right]=-\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{0},X_{t}}\left[\frac{1}{t}\sum_{i\in\{1,2,\dots,L\},X_{t}^{i}=[M]}\log p_{\theta}(X_{0}^{i}|X_{t})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{\theta}(X_{0}|X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{\theta}(X_{0}|X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is factorized into <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{i=1}^{L}p_{\theta}(X_{0}^{i}|X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{i=1}^{L}p_{\theta}(X_{0}^{i}|X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> based on independence assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. At inference time, the model starts from a fully masked sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=[M,M,\dots,M]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=[M,M,\dots,M]</annotation></semantics></math> and progressively applies the learned reverse process <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{\theta}(X_{0}|X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{\theta}(X_{0}|X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> to recover the original tokens. We provide a more detailed formulation of MDMs in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Formulation of Masked Diffusion Models ‣ Appendix A Additional Technical Details ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Unified Multi-modal Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unified multi-modal models such as GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite> are capable of both image understanding and generation tasks, leading to strong performance on tasks requiring both capabilities, such as image editing. Generally, there are two dominant types of unified models based on their modeling objectives. The first type employs an autoregressive next-token prediction objective for text generation and a diffusion objective for image generation (AR+diff). Notable models in this category include BLIP-3o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, BAGEL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and Metaqueries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, this design involves two different training objectives with distinct numerical scales and training dynamics, often requiring careful tuning of loss weighting and data mixtures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, the second type of models employ a unified objectives for both image and texts. Early works like Janus-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025c</a>)</cite> employ a unified AR modeling objective. Several recent works like MMaDa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> explore a unified masked diffusion objective. Despite some success, a significant performance gap remains between these unified MDMs and state-of-the-art unified models in the AR and AR+diff categories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Architecturally, unified models also fall into two main categories. The first type, such as Janus and MMaDa, uses a single dense transformer to output both image and text tokens. The second type, such as BAGEL and MetaQueries, employs separate parameter sets for handling image and text modalities. A common design in this category is the mixture-of-transformers (MoT) architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, where image and text inputs are processed by different parameter sets but can interact through joint attention mechanisms. These designs are illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1.1 Elastic Mixture-of-Transformers (ElasticMoT) ‣ 3.1 Model Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. While being more flexible, training MoT experts can be expensive due to the duplication of parameters. For example, BAGEL extends a 7B Qwen model into a 7B+7B MoT, doubling the parameter count.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.19244v1/Artboard_42.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/239;" width="476" height="239" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Overall Pipeline of Lavida-O.<span id="S3.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Given an input image and text prompt, we first concatenate the image semantic embedding <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, image VQ embedding <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, and text prompt embedding <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to form the conditioning embedding <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. The combined embedding is then passed to the model alongside the partially masked sequence <math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The model then predicts the fully-unmasked sequence <math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Model Architecture</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lavida-O’s model architecture is built on LaViDa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, a diffusion vision-language model capable of image understanding tasks. LaViDa uses a SigLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> vision encoder to convert input images into continuous semantic embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are concatenated with token embeddings of text prompts <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to form the final conditional embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\text{Concat}(C_{i},C_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\text{Concat}(C_{i},C_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> for visual understanding tasks. At each inference step, the diffusion model uses the partially unmasked answer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the conditional embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to predict the reverse distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(X_{0}|X_{t},C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(X_{0}|X_{t},C)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the clean text answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For image understanding tasks, Lavida-O maintains this eact setup of LaViDa. To incorporate visual generation tasks, we extend LaViDa’s design by representing target images as sequences of discrete tokens using a VQ-Encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Esser et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. When performing these tasks, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> contain not only text tokens, but also VQ tokens representing images. For image editing and interleaved generation tasks, we additionally incorporate VQ tokens of input images <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> as part of the conditional embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\text{Concat}(C_{i},C_{v},C_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\text{Concat}(C_{i},C_{v},C_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, since using semantic embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> alone may lose low-level details needed for editing. To reduce the number of tokens and improve computational efficiency, we introduce a token compression module that reduces the number of VQ tokens by a factor of 4. The overall pipeline is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Elastic Mixture-of-Transformers (ElasticMoT)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to find an effective and efficient method that can equip LaViDa, an understanding-only diffusion model, with visual generation capabilities. However, both of the existing common choices described in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Unified Multi-modal Models ‣ 2 Background and Related Works ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>—dense models and MoT—are very expensive. Dense models use the same set of parameters for both understanding and generation tasks, requiring a mix of understanding and generation data during training to prevent catastrophic forgetting, which is not data-efficient. While the MoT setup allows freezing the understanding branch and training only the generation branch for image generation, its architecture doubles the total parameter count, leading to considerable computational overhead. Moreover, given an 8B base understanding model, both setups require training at least 8B parameters for generation tasks from scratch, which is prohibitively expensive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we propose Elastic-MoT, a novel architecture design that efficiently adapts an understanding-only model for image generation tasks. Compared with the vanilla MoT architecture, Elastic-MoT introduces two major modifications. First, instead of using equally sized understanding and generation branches, the generation branch has a smaller hidden size. This reduces the parameter count and enables efficient training. We make this design choice based on the observation that many text-to-image models can generate high-quality images with only 2–4B parameters, suggesting that generation tasks may not require as much capacity as understanding tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, given an <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-layer model, instead of having joint attention at all layers, we only allow text and image modalities to interact in the first <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> layers. In the remaining <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=N-M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=N-M</annotation></semantics></math> layers, text and image tokens interact only within their modality through self-attention. This design activates only partial parameters for different tasks. For example, in Lavida-O’s final design, the generation branch consists of 2.4B new parameters, while the understanding branch has 8B parameters initialized from LaViDa. With <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=32</annotation></semantics></math> layers, we set <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=K=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=K=16</annotation></semantics></math>. For image generation tasks, we activate all 2.4B parameters in the image generation branch and 4B parameters from the first 16 layers of the understanding branch, leading to 6.4B active parameters. During text-to-image pretraining, we train only the 2.4B parameters in the generation branch, and omit loading the last 16 layers of the understanding branch since they are unused. This enables efficient scaling of text-to-image pretraining without introducing extra understanding data or bearing the large parameter count of MoT. Similarly, the active parameter count is 8B for understanding tasks, and 10.4B for tasks requiring both understanding and generation, such as interleaved generation. The full design of Elastic-MoT is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1.1 Elastic Mixture-of-Transformers (ElasticMoT) ‣ 3.1 Model Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We provide additional details and analysis in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Elastic-MoT Architecture ‣ Appendix A Additional Technical Details ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> and <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Ablation Studies on Elastic-MoT Design ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiment Details and Results ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19244v1/Artboard_7.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/217;" width="476" height="217"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Design of Elastic MoT.<span id="S3.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Elastic-MoT introduces two major modifications to standard MoT. First, the generation branch has a smaller hidden size. Second, given an <math id="S3.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-layer model, we only allow text and image modalities to interact in the first <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> layers. These two designs allow us to flexibly load only a portion of parameters depending on tasks, improving the efficiency.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Modality-aware Masking</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of the challenges in adapting MoT architecture for MDMs is routing—the mechanism to determine which branch should be activated for each token. This is trivial for unified AR MoT models, where the model can simply learn to generate a special token (e.g., [img_start]) to indicate that the next token should use the generation branch. When the last token of an image is generated, the model automatically switches back to the understanding branch to continue text generation. However, this approach is not feasible for MDMs, which decode tokens in parallel and must decide in advance which mask tokens should be routed to the understanding branch and which to the generation branch. A naive solution is to let the user specify the number and location of text and image tokens, but this is difficult for interleaved generation, such as image generation with self-reflection. To address this issue, we design a modality-aware masking process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a sequence of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> text tokens and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> image VQ tokens, the vanilla forward diffusion process gradually converts it into <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M+N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M+N</annotation></semantics></math> mask tokens during the time interval <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. By contrast, our modality-aware forward process introduces a special timestamp <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{exp}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{exp}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, at which fully masked image VQ tokens are collapsed into a special <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\text{exp}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>exp</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\text{exp}]</annotation></semantics></math> text token. This process is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 3.1.2 Modality-aware Masking ‣ 3.1 Model Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> (Bottom-up). At inference, we assume all mask tokens are text tokens at the beginning. When a <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\text{exp}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>exp</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\text{exp}]</annotation></semantics></math> token is generated, we replace it with a sequence of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math> mask tokens, and specify that these tokens will be processed by the generation branch for image synthesis in subsequent forward calls. <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by a pre-specified image resolution. This process is also illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 3.1.2 Modality-aware Masking ‣ 3.1 Model Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> (Top-down). We provide additional details in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Modality-Aware Masking ‣ Appendix A Additional Technical Details ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19244v1/Modality-Aware-Masking-6.svg" id="S3.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/373;" width="476" height="373"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Modality-Aware Masking </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19244v1/sampling-stratified-main-2.svg" id="S3.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Stratified Sampling </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Design choices of Lavida-O<span id="S3.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. (a) Forward diffusion process with modality-aware masking. (b) Visualization of the unmasking order in the proposed stratified random sampling process.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Task-Specific Designs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe several additional technical innovations that improve the effectiveness and efficiency on newly incorporated tasks such as image generation, image editing and grounding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Universal Text Conditioning</span>. A common approach to improving the quality of text-to-image models is micro-conditioning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Podell et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which conditions the image generation process on extra parameters such as original image resolution, crop coordinates, and image quality scores. This is typically achieved via specialized embeddings or modulation layers. However, since a unified model has strong language understanding and reasoning capabilities, we can simply append these conditions as plain text (e.g., “SCORE: 5.40”) to the end of user prompts. In addition to common conditions, we also incorporate image luminance and contrast as micro-conditions. This simple and effective design not only improves image quality by biasing generation toward high-scoring distributions, but also gives users more refined control over outputs. We provide additional details in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Universal Text Conditioning ‣ Appendix A Additional Technical Details ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stratified Random Sampling.</span> Existing MDMs mostly adopt confidence-based sampling at inference, where tokens with high confidence scores are unmasked first. However, this design is suboptimal for image generation, where many tokens need to be decoded at each step. In particular, high-confidence tokens tend to cluster in small spatial regions adjacent to already unmasked tokens. This negatively affects image quality, since adjacent tokens are highly correlated, yet MDMs sample all unmasked tokens independently and assume their joint distribution perfectly factorizes. To mitigate this, we aim to minimize correlation between unmasked tokens at each step by maximizing their spatial distance. We achieve this with a stratified random sampling process, which ensures unmasked tokens are evenly distributed across the image. Specifically, we first divide the image into <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> square regions and unmask one random token per region. We then subdivide each region into 4 subregions, giving 16 in total. Four already contain an unmasked token; we randomly unmask one token from each of the remaining 12. We repeat this process until all tokens are unmasked. This is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 3.1.2 Modality-aware Masking ‣ 3.1 Model Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>. More details and analysis are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Stratified Random Sampling ‣ Appendix A Additional Technical Details ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> and <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Ablation Studies on Stratified Sampling ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiment Details and Results ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Planning and Reasoning.</span> While existing unified MDMs integrate image understanding and generation tasks with a single diffusion objective, they do not incorporate mechanisms that use understanding to improve generation, except for the assumption that joint training benefits both tasks. To address this, we introduce two explicit mechanisms that leverage understanding to improve generation: <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">planning</em> and <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reflection</em>. With planning, the model first generates a layout of the image represented by bounding boxes, then creates the actual image accordingly. For image editing tasks, it first identifies the desired edit region before generating the edited image. With reflection, the model evaluates its own generation using its understanding capability and determines whether it satisfies the user’s request. If misalignment is detected, the model generates a new image correcting the error. Examples are shown in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, with additional results in the appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object Grounding with Coordinate Quantization</span>. The bi-directional context of MDMs naturally allows parallel decoding of bounding box coordinates. While Lavida-O can represent numbers as plain text, we adopt a specialized scheme that normalizes all bounding box coordinates to <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and quantizes them into 1025 discrete tokens representing <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{0}{1024},\frac{1}{1024},...,\frac{1024}{1024}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>0</mn><mn>1024</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>1024</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1024</mn><mn>1024</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{0}{1024},\frac{1}{1024},...,\frac{1024}{1024}</annotation></semantics></math>. This ensures each bounding box is represented by exactly four tokens. At inference, we construct a multiple query input with masked tokens such as “A dog [m][m][m][m]; A cat [m][m][m][m]”, and unmask all coordinates in parallel. This design allow us to decode multiple bounding boxes in a single diffusion step, greatly boosting the efficiency. We provide further details in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS6" title="A.6 Object Grounding with Coordinate Quantization ‣ Appendix A Additional Technical Details ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start with LaViDa, an 8B understanding-only diffusion model, and extend it with a 2.4B image generation branch using the ElasticMoT architecture described in Section <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS1" title="3.1.1 Elastic Mixture-of-Transformers (ElasticMoT) ‣ 3.1 Model Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.1</span></a>. The training consists of three stages:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1:</span> We continue training the base model on object grounding and image-level understanding tasks using 12M samples. In this stage, the generation branch is not activated.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2:</span> We pretrain the 2.4B image generation branch for text-to-image generation tasks using 200M image-text pairs. In this stage, we load 2.4B + 4B parameters using the ElasticMoT design described in Section <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS1" title="3.1.1 Elastic Mixture-of-Transformers (ElasticMoT) ‣ 3.1 Model Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.1</span></a>. We freeze the understanding branch and train only the generation branch, thereby avoiding the need to include understanding data to prevent forgetting. To further improve training efficiency, we start at a resolution of 256 and progressively increase it to 512 and 1024 during training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 3:</span> In the final stage, we jointly train the entire 2.4B + 8B model end-to-end on image-level understanding, text-to-image generation, image editing, and interleaved generation tasks such as planning and self-reflection. In addition to the data used in previous stages, we incorporate 1.5M image editing samples and 3M interleaved generation samples.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More details about the training data and process are provided in Apendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Setup ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiment Details and Results ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Image Understanding</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report performance on image understanding tasks in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Image Understanding ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Lavida-O: Elastic Masked Diffusion Models for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Lavida-O outperforms the previous state-of-the-art unified diffusion model, MMaDa, by a considerable margin on MMMU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, MME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and MMB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>. Compared with the base model LaViDa, Lavida-O achieves substantial improvements on most benchmarks such as ChartQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Masry et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, DocVQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathew et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, ScienceQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and MathVista <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, thanks to the scaling of training data.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Quantitative results on image-level understanding tasks<span id="S4.T2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. *We evaluated results not reported in the original paper using official weights.</span></span></figcaption>
